 forces out this year  i probably would have taken the same  position as  general austin or as secretary austin  and the joint chiefs of staff and  recommending uh  that we keep our small contingent there  but it was a very tough decision  and and the fact is even with our forces  there  uh the taliban are gaining ground every  single day  taking more territory and and so frankly  of all the possible endings  at this point in afghanistan a happy  ending is probably uh  is probably the least likely the one  thing that we have to do it seems to me  given the decisions that have been made  you know when  the soviets pulled their troops out  their  government the government they installed  of najibullah actually lasted for three  years  as long as soviet military and economic  assistance kept flowing  but once the soviet union collapsed the  najibullah government collapsed  so it's absolutely critical now that  this decision is made and we are pulling  our troops out  that we continue the flow of economic  and military assistance to to the afghan  government  otherwise i think they have little  prospect of success or of survival mr  secretary  we just had this piece by jennifer  griffin at the pentagon about the afghan  translators some 17 000 of them as u.s  troops leave  they fear that their lives are in  jeopardy what should we do about them  should we evacuate them  well i think we have an obligation to  them and we need to get them out  and frankly i think the way we've  treated a lot of the  interpreters that work with in  afghanistan  has been a disgrace we've been able to  get some out but not nearly enough  these people's lives are going to be at  risk  in afghanistan their lives and the lives  of their families they work for america  they risk their lives for our troops uh  we have an obligation to them and and  and if we fail to follow through on that  obligation then shame on us  uh you know in your last book uh duty  memoirs of the secretary of war uh you  wrote this about joe biden i think he  has been wrong on nearly every major  foreign policy and national security  issue over the past four decades  i asked you about that back in june of  2020 here's what you said  i stand by the statement that i wrote in  the book that  i obviously have very significant policy  differences with  with joe biden but i do think that he's  a  person of integrity and  a decent person and i think i'll just  leave it at that  do you sense that his judgment on  foreign policy is going to change now  that he's president  well what i found interesting is the is  the degree of continuity  in policy between the trump  administration and the biden  administration particularly when it  comes to  china and and russia uh  i think that the one change that i would  applaud  is is the recognition of the importance  uh in our in achieving our objectives of  uh taking advantage of our allies and  our alliances  uh and having them uh in concert with us  in  uh following policies dealing with china  as well as with  uh as well as with russia and vladimir  putin  but in those two very significant areas  it seems to me that there's a lot of  continuity with what went before  i think i think you're seeing a fair  amount of continuity  with respect to north korea they talk  about threading the needle between  what the obama administration did and  what the  trump administration did uh that  pretty much sounds like more of the same  to me right iran is definitely a  different story and how this is a  different story  i want to ask you about that in a second  but just uh back on um  the past time for then vice president  biden i just want to  straighten one thing out that has come  up we just celebrated i should celebrate  it we  marked the 10-year uh mark of the  bin laden raid uh that took down osama  bin laden remember that situation room  picture that is so famous you are in  there uh  and there is a book uh mark bowden on  the bin laden raid  the finish the killing of osama bin  laden and it says  in the end every one of the president's  top advisers except  biden was in favor of taking immediate  action  is that true it is  for uh for a good part of the debate  uh i i actually preferred i had no doubt  about the ability of the seals to carry  out the mission  but i preferred the use of a drone my my  worry  was given my responsibilities as  secretary of defense my worry was that  whether or not the  the raid was successful the pakistani  reaction would be so severe  that they would cut off our supply line  between karachi  and afghanistan and we would lose the  war in afghanistan almost overnight  but a couple of days before the raid i  was persuaded uh  that that the raid was the best  alternative and  was called the national security adviser  and told him that i that i supported  going forward with the raid biden was  not persuaded  that's my understanding  i said i was going to ask you about iran  there's a new defense intelligence  agency report  that says al-qaeda is during the past  year counter-terrorism pressure has  eliminated parts of al-qaeda's senior  leadership  overall ayman al-zawahiri remains in  hiding while a handful of iran-based  al-qaeda leaders oversee al qaeda's  network  inside iran this as obviously iran's  thumbing its nose at the u.s  enriching uranium to 60 percent still  funding terrorist operations in the  middle east  and the administration is trying to sit  down and renegotiate this deal  your thoughts on the juxtaposition there  of all of that  yeah i think we ought not have any  illusions about the iranian regime and  and uh and the people who are who are  running it and making the decisions one  of the points that i make in the book  exercise of power is that repeatedly  u.s administrations every u.s  administration has reached out and tried  to  improve relations with the iranian  government  and in every case even where you had the  iranian president  supporting such a move the ayatollah  put the kibosh on it and so we need to  understand that reality i i think that  my my concern is trying to go back to  the agreement  as it existed in 2015 half the time has  already gone  i was heartened for a while when they  were talking about lengthening and  strengthening in the agreement  i think i think we need an agreement  that says that the iranians will never  have a nuclear weapon we need  improved monitoring or verification  capabilities and we clearly need  something  that deals with their ballistic missile  programs and finally i would say  that if we're going to pursue these  negotiations the one thing we  must do differently than the obama  administration  is complete transparency and inclusion  of  the israelis and our gulf allies  and friends so that they know we're not  going to do a deal behind their back  that will be harmful to their security  interests  mr secretary we appreciate the time of  the book exercise of power  it's a great read about the past and the  future thanks a lot  thank you brett up next hey sean hannity  here hey  click here to subscribe to fox news  youtube page  and catch our hottest interviews and  most compelling analysis  you will not get it anywhere else
 

